{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>Behold, this day they offered their sin-offering, etc.</b> Moses picked a quarrel with Elazar and Itamar: why did you not eat the sin-offering? And Aaron defended himself and said: ״וְאָכַלְתִּי חַטָּאת הַיּוֹם״ — and it appears to me according to the words of Mahar[i]a that this goat that was burned was the goat of the sin-offering mentioned above, which was קָדְשֵׁי שָׁעָה, and therefore Aaron did not wish to eat of it, because he reasoned by the logic of the matter that the priests eat and the owners achieve atonement when the priests have no share in that transgression; but this goat, which was an atonement for the matter of the calf that they sacrificed there — their sacrifices to goats, as explained above in the verse ״קַח לְךָ עֵגֶל״ — and this sin Aaron had caused, so it is as if he had a share in it; therefore there is no justice in Aaron eating of it so that the owners be atoned, for it is as if he too were among the owners. And since the sons of Aaron saw that their father did not wish to eat, therefore they too did not eat of it and burned it." "And this is the explanation of the matter: Moses was angry with Elazar and Itamar, the ones remaining alive from the death of Nadab and Abihu, who were burned because they offered strange fire. And Moses thought that these too had offered strange fire, for since the law of the sin-offering is to be eaten and they had burned it, surely this fire in which they burned it is strange fire. Therefore he was angry with them and said: you should have remembered what happened to your brothers, and why were you spared? And you should have eaten it, for ״אוֹתָהּ נָתַן לָכֶם לָשֵׂאת אֶת עֲוֹן הָעֵדָה״ — that the priests eat and the owners achieve atonement. ״וַיְדַבֵּר אַהֲרֹן אֶל מֹשֶׁה הֵן הַיּוֹם הִקְרִיבוּ״ etc. — he wished to demonstrate by a clear sign that this sin-offering was on account of the matter of the calf, for if not so, how is it possible that on this very day, when my sons offered the people's sin-offering and burnt-offering to atone for them, such things should befall me — that on that very day two of my sons should die? For if they were already worthy of being struck down and the Holy One, blessed be He, waited for them so as not to mix in the joy of the Torah, He should have waited also this day, which was the joy of the heart of the Holy One, blessed be He, at the dedication of the altar. Rather, surely HaShem desired that on this day the sin of the calf be wiped out entirely from Israel and from Aaron on one day: for Aaron pays back double to his fellow, and Israel achieve atonement through the sin-offering and the burnt-offering. And this is a clear sign that the sin-offering came on account of the sin of the calf. And if so: if I had eaten of this sin-offering today, would it have been good in the eyes of HaShem? Surely I too am among the owners; and since I did not eat, my sons too did not wish to eat it out of honor for me. And this thing was good in the eyes of Moses."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "The abbreviation מַהֲרִי״א is expanded as Mahar[i]a, left partially unresolved as the text writes only the consonantal abbreviation without vowel indicators; the context (interpreting the goat-sin-offering) does not disambiguate which specific figure named Mahar[i]a is intended."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:35:38.903539346Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}